Use subset-sum dynamic programming, updating sums downward so each element is used at most once. Empty input returns true.

```javascript
function solve(nums) {
  const total = nums.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0);
  if (total % 2 !== 0) return false;

  const target = total / 2;
  const reachable = new Array(target + 1).fill(false);
  reachable[0] = true;

  for (const value of nums) {
    for (let sum = target; sum >= value; sum--) {
      reachable[sum] = reachable[sum] || reachable[sum - value];
    }
  }

  return reachable[target];
}
```

Call solve(nums) directly; no imports, exports, or stdin/stdout are needed. For n values and target T = total / 2, time is O(n(T + 1)) and auxiliary space is O(T + 1).

