Use a rolling row of path counts; blocked cells reset their count to zero.

```javascript
function solve(grid) {
  if (grid.length === 0 || grid[0].length === 0) return 0;

  const columns = grid[0].length;
  const dp = new Array(columns).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (const row of grid) {
    for (let col = 0; col < columns; col++) {
      if (row[col] === 1) {
        dp[col] = 0;
      } else if (col > 0) {
        dp[col] += dp[col - 1];
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[columns - 1];
}
```

Call solve(grid) directly with a rectangular numeric 0/1 grid. Time: O(rows × columns). Auxiliary space: O(columns).

